  "reason": "The records provide several ingredients pointing generally toward the later result: rapid cooling from a melt can kinetically retain metastable phases (R10), cooling history can control secondary hexagonal-phase formation (R11), as-cast solidification can generate compositionally segregated dual-phase HEAs (R16), and C14 Laves phases can coexist with solid-solution matrices after solidification (R13, R17, R19, R20). R5 also establishes the related magnetic principle that antiferromagnetic constituents can reduce the saturation magnetization of Fe/Co-based HEAs, while R9 and R12 provide nonmagnetic analogies for interface or second-phase pinning. However, none identifies FeCoNiTi, the specific fcc/C14 segregation, a compositionally matched C14-dominant control, Ti-sublattice antiferromagnetic coupling, orbital-moment-assisted coercivity, magnetic domain-wall pinning by C14/spin-canted components, or the assignment of the 168 K, 214 K, and 1084 K transitions. Thus the literature points toward cooling-controlled multiphase design and related magnetic effects, but reconstructing the specific intervention and mechanism would still require a substantial new idea."
